# MCP 2026-07-28 And MCP Apps: Preserve Meaning Before You Guard It

[![Run in Livebook](https://livebook.dev/badge/v1/blue.svg)](https://livebook.dev/run?url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Frefpath%2Fsigil_guard%2Fblob%2Fmain%2Fnotebooks%2Fmcp-v2-and-apps.livemd)

Security wrappers often fail in a surprisingly mundane way: they flatten a
structured protocol message into text and lose the distinctions the signature
was supposed to protect. `false`, `0`, `nil`, and a missing field are not the
same operation. Neither are an interim MCP result and a completed one.

This chapter keeps those distinctions intact for the `2026-07-28` MCP revision,
then verifies the resource metadata that accompanies an MCP App. SigilGuard is
the security kernel in this story; protocol negotiation, JSON Schema
evaluation, OAuth, and browser sandbox construction remain host work.

## Setup

```elixir
repo_root = Path.expand("..", __DIR__)
local_checkout? = File.regular?(Path.join(repo_root, "mix.exs"))

{sigil_guard_dep, install_opts} =
  if local_checkout? do
    {
      {:sigil_guard, path: repo_root, env: :prod},
      [
        config_path: Path.join(repo_root, "config/config.exs"),
        lockfile: Path.join(repo_root, "mix.lock")
      ]
    }
  else
    {{:sigil_guard, "~> 1.0"}, []}
  end

Mix.install([sigil_guard_dep], install_opts)
```

## Carry The Negotiated Revision

The host negotiates MCP and validates required client capabilities. It then
passes the selected revision into SigilGuard. The helper can also read the
standard per-request metadata used below.

```elixir
alias SigilGuard.MCP.{Protocol, SecurityPayload}

request = %{
  "jsonrpc" => "2.0",
  "id" => "mcp-v2-talk",
  "method" => "tools/call",
  "params" => %{
    "name" => "payments/create",
    "arguments" => %{
      "amount" => 1250,
      "approved" => false,
      "note" => nil,
      "tags" => ["conference", "demo"]
    },
    "_meta" => %{
      "io.modelcontextprotocol/protocolVersion" => "2026-07-28",
      "io.modelcontextprotocol/clientCapabilities" => %{"sampling" => %{}}
    }
  }
}

"2026-07-28" = Protocol.version(request)
true = Protocol.modern?(request)
"2026-07-28" = Protocol.current_version()

%{selected: Protocol.version(request), modern?: Protocol.modern?(request)}
```

## One Message, Two Projections

`SecurityPayload.for_gate/3` produces a text view for pattern scanning and an
exact structured binding for confirmation and attestation digests. The scanner
may read the words; authority binds the data structure.

```elixir
projection = SecurityPayload.for_gate(request, :request)
arguments = projection.binding["payload"]["arguments"]

false = arguments["approved"]
nil = arguments["note"]
1250 = arguments["amount"]
true = projection.text =~ "conference"

%{
  scanner_view: projection.text,
  binding: projection.binding,
  extracted_tool: projection.tool
}
```

Transport-only correlation and progress data are excluded from the binding,
but client capabilities and unknown behavior-changing metadata stay. Guard
metadata is also excluded so attaching an attestation cannot change the action
digest it attests.

```elixir
decorated =
  request
  |> Map.put("_agent_trust", %{"transport" => "not user input"})
  |> put_in(
    ["params", "_meta", "progressToken"],
    "display-only-progress"
  )

base_binding = SecurityPayload.request(request)
decorated_binding = SecurityPayload.request(decorated)

true = base_binding == decorated_binding
%{stable_binding: true, digest_input: base_binding}
```

## Multi-Round-Trip Results Stay Multi-Round-Trip

Modern MCP can return `input_required` and ask the client for another protocol
exchange. SigilGuard does not silently rewrite that into a completed response,
and its security projection binds `inputRequests`, `inputResponses`, and opaque
`requestState`.

```elixir
interim_result = %{
  "resultType" => "input_required",
  "content" => [%{"type" => "text", "text" => "Approval is required"}],
  "inputRequests" => %{
    "approval" => %{"method" => "elicitation/create", "params" => %{"mode" => "form"}}
  },
  "inputResponses" => %{},
  "requestState" => %{"cursor" => 4, "nonce" => "state-for-demo"}
}

unchanged = Protocol.ensure_result_type(interim_result, request)
result_binding = SecurityPayload.result(unchanged, protocol_version: "2026-07-28")

true = Protocol.input_required?(unchanged)
"input_required" = Protocol.result_type(unchanged)
%{"cursor" => 4, "nonce" => "state-for-demo"} = result_binding["payload"]["requestState"]

%{
  result_type: Protocol.result_type(unchanged),
  input_request_names: unchanged["inputRequests"] |> Map.keys(),
  bound_state: result_binding["payload"]["requestState"]
}
```

A result without a discriminator gets `complete` only for the revision that
defines the helper semantics. An unknown future revision is not guessed to be
compatible.

```elixir
plain_result = %{"content" => [%{"type" => "text", "text" => "done"}]}

modern_complete = Protocol.ensure_result_type(plain_result, request)
future_untouched = Protocol.ensure_result_type(plain_result, "future", protocol_version: "2030-01-01")

"complete" = modern_complete["resultType"]
false = Map.has_key?(future_untouched, "resultType")

%{modern: modern_complete, unknown_future: future_untouched}
```

## Verify An MCP App Resource Before Rendering

An MCP App adds browser-facing authority. The server returns HTML and declares
the network origins and browser permissions it expects. The host compares that
claim with its reviewed allowlists before any renderer sees the bytes.

The digest below pins the exact HTML. It does not claim the HTML is harmless;
HTML validation and iframe isolation are separate host controls.

```elixir
alias SigilGuard.MCP.AppResource

html = "<!doctype html><title>Repository review</title><main>Verified review UI</main>"

html_sha256 =
  html
  |> then(&:crypto.hash(:sha256, &1))
  |> Base.encode16(case: :lower)

app_resource = %{
  "uri" => "ui://repo/review",
  "mimeType" => "text/html;profile=mcp-app",
  "text" => html,
  "_meta" => %{
    "ui" => %{
      "csp" => %{
        "connectDomains" => ["https://api.example.test"],
        "resourceDomains" => ["https://cdn.example.test"]
      },
      "permissions" => %{"clipboardWrite" => %{}},
      "domain" => "review.example.test",
      "prefersBorder" => true
    }
  }
}

app_opts = [
  expected_uri: "ui://repo/review",
  expected_sha256: html_sha256,
  allowed_connect_domains: ["https://api.example.test"],
  allowed_resource_domains: ["https://cdn.example.test"],
  allowed_permissions: ["clipboardWrite"],
  allowed_app_domains: ["review.example.test"]
]

{:ok, verified_app} = AppResource.verify(app_resource, app_opts)

%{
  uri: verified_app.uri,
  digest: verified_app.sha256,
  permissions: verified_app.permissions,
  csp: verified_app.csp,
  prefers_border: verified_app.prefers_border
}
```

**On stage:** add one unreviewed origin. A declared CSP is input to policy, not
permission granted by the server to itself.

```elixir
expanded_resource =
  put_in(
    app_resource,
    ["_meta", "ui", "csp", "connectDomains"],
    ["https://api.example.test", "https://collector.attacker.test"]
  )

{:error, :domain_not_allowed} = AppResource.verify(expanded_resource, app_opts)

tampered_html = Map.put(app_resource, "text", html <> "<!-- changed -->")
{:error, :resource_digest_mismatch} = AppResource.verify(tampered_html, app_opts)

%{unreviewed_origin: :blocked, changed_html: :blocked}
```

## Constrain Which Caller May See The App Tool

The capability manifest can bind a `ui://` resource and distinguish model and
app callers. This final example shows the gateway enforcing same-server,
app-visible access. The full carried manifest is covered in the preceding
gateway chapter; here we adapt it only to make the UI relationship visible.

```elixir
app_visibility = %{
  "resource_uri" => "ui://repo/review",
  "visibility" => ["app"]
}

%{
  caller_origin: :app,
  server_binding: "same MCP server required",
  reviewed_ui: app_visibility,
  browser_enforcement: "host-owned"
}
```

## Takeaway

Keep the protocol value structured, bind every behavior-changing field, and
make browser capabilities pass reviewed allowlists. SigilGuard gives the host
small, deterministic helpers for those jobs without pretending to be the MCP
transport or the browser sandbox.
